## Why This Is Asked

This question probes end-to-end design of a multi-tenant LLM-ops pipeline balancing latency, privacy, policy management, and observability. It assesses ability to design streaming prompts, per-tenant models, hot-updatable policies, drift alerts, and tests.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy engine
- Data locality and privacy controls
- Streaming prompt handling
- Dynamic model routing
- Drift detection and audits
- Tamper-evident logs and auditable trails

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: pick a model based on policy
function selectModel(tenant, policy) {
  const p = policy[tenant] || {};
  if (p.forceGuard) return 'safety-guarded';
  if (p.latencyBudget && p.latencyBudget < 75) return 'base';
  return p.preferredModel ?? 'base';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate drift triggers in production without constant retraining?
- What would be your approach to simulate hot policy updates during peak load?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical skills in enforcing privacy, security, and auditability in a multi-tenant LLM-ops gateway. It requires concrete architecture and measurable guarantees beyond high-level design.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy enforcement
- Deterministic validation and replayability
- PII detection and redaction
- Prompt-injection guardrails
- Latency budgeting and tamper-evident logging

## Code Example

```javascript
function validatePrompt(prompt, policy) {
  // normalize
  let p = prompt.trim();
  // redaction rules (pseudo)
  policy.redactionPatterns.forEach(r => p = p.replace(r, 'REDACTED'));
  // injection guard (very simple placeholder)
  const blocked = policy.injectionPatterns.some(pat => new RegExp(pat, 'i').test(p));
  return { valid: !blocked, redacted: p };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test policy coverage across tenants with different privacy rules?
- How would you handle policy updates without affecting in-flight prompts?

